WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.250
Linux AI GPU reliability depends on the full stack: hardware, firmware, kernel modules, user-space libraries, display or compute components, containers, model runtimes, and applications.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:17.026
A GPU being physically present is only the beginning.

00:00:17.026 --> 00:00:24.791
A usable acceleration stack must be compatible and validated from the point where the workload actually runs.

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:35.476
Strong practice uses planned versions, documented configurations, compatibility checks, validation tests, monitoring, backups, and controlled updates.

00:00:35.476 --> 00:00:47.017
Driver versions, kernels, CUDA or ROCm components, container images, Python environments, and inference runtimes should be treated as related parts of one system.

00:00:47.017 --> 00:00:50.650
Random version mixing makes troubleshooting harder.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.784
Validation should prove more than detection.

00:00:53.784 --> 00:01:07.391
Check driver load status, management tools, GPU visibility inside containers or runtimes, simple inference smoke tests, performance behavior, utilization, memory, temperature, and throttling.

00:01:07.391 --> 00:01:17.578
Use those checks to separate driver issues from model size, context length, runtime settings, permission problems, and container configuration.

00:01:17.578 --> 00:01:21.282
The goal is not simply to install the newest driver.

00:01:21.282 --> 00:01:27.765
The goal is to build a stable, repeatable acceleration stack that supports the AI workload.

00:01:27.765 --> 00:01:34.532
A well-managed Linux GPU system is documented, recoverable, monitored, and updated with intent.

00:01:34.532 --> 00:01:40.445
That is what makes local AI, inference servers, and development systems dependable.
